\section{Pipeline Overview}
An expanded step-by-step pipeline:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Data Import}: Read raw .idat files using \texttt{read.metharray.exp()} to create an \texttt{RGChannelSet}.
    \item \textbf{Annotation}: Use the cleaned manifest file (\texttt{Illumina450Manifest\_clean.RData}) to map probe IDs and design types.
    \item \textbf{Quality Control}: Calculate detection p-values (\texttt{detectionP()}) and generate quality control plots (\texttt{plotQC()}, \texttt{controlStripPlot()}).
    \item \textbf{Preprocessing}: Apply background correction with \texttt{preprocessRaw()} and functional normalization with \texttt{preprocessFunnorm()}.
    \item \textbf{Beta/M-value Calculation}: Calculate Beta-values (\texttt{getBeta()}) and M-values (\texttt{getM()}).
    \item \textbf{Statistical Testing}: Perform t-tests (\texttt{t.test()}) comparing groups.
    \item \textbf{Multiple Testing Correction}: Apply correction for multiple testing using \texttt{p.adjust()} (methods: BH, Bonferroni).
    \item \textbf{Visualization}: Generate density plots, boxplots, PCA plots (\texttt{prcomp()}, \texttt{fviz\_eig()}), volcano plots, Manhattan plots (\texttt{qqman}), and heatmaps (\texttt{heatmap.2()}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The Analytical Pipeline — From Biological Question to Visualization}
\noindent
A successful DNA methylation study requires a well-designed workflow that systematically addresses every analytical step. Below, we outline a typical pipeline, emphasizing its logical flow from hypothesis formulation to data visualization:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Biological Question}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Define the scientific goal, e.g., “Does methylation differ between disease and control groups?”
        \item Establish hypotheses and experimental design (number of samples, replicates, conditions).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Sample Collection and Preparation}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Extract DNA from relevant tissues or cell types.
        \item Perform bisulfite conversion to differentiate methylated from unmethylated cytosines.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Data Acquisition}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Use the Illumina HumanMethylation450K array (or EPIC) to profile methylation.
        \item Generate raw data files (.idat) containing red and green channel intensities.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Data Import into R}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Use \texttt{read.metharray.exp()} to read .idat files into an \texttt{RGChannelSet} object.
        \item Inspect sample metadata (e.g., group, sex, batch) from \texttt{SampleSheet.csv}.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Annotation}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Link probe IDs to genomic features using the cleaned manifest (\texttt{Illumina450Manifest
        \_clean.RData}).
        \item Exclude problematic probes (e.g., SNP-overlapping, cross-hybridizing).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Quality Control}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Calculate detection p-values with \texttt{detectionP()} to filter unreliable probes.
        \item Assess sample quality using \texttt{plotQC()} and \texttt{controlStripPlot()} for negative controls.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Preprocessing}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Apply background correction using \texttt{preprocessRaw()}.
        \item Normalize data using functional normalization with \texttt{preprocessFunnorm()} to mitigate technical biases and batch effects.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Methylation Quantification}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Calculate Beta-values with \texttt{getBeta()} and M-values with \texttt{getM()}.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Statistical Testing}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Perform t-tests for group comparisons (\texttt{t.test()}).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Multiple Testing Correction}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Correct p-values for multiple testing using \texttt{p.adjust()} (methods: BH and Bonferroni).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Visualization}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Generate density plots, boxplots, PCA plots (\texttt{prcomp()}, \texttt{fviz\_eig()}), volcano plots, Manhattan plots (\texttt{qqman}), and heatmaps (\texttt{heatmap.2()}).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Biological Interpretation}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Map significant CpGs to genes and pathways.
        \item Integrate methylation findings with external datasets (e.g., gene expression, clinical data).
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent
This pipeline illustrates how each step builds upon the previous one, transforming raw experimental data into biologically meaningful insights.

\section{Interpretation of Outputs and Parameter Choices}

A high-level technical discussion that complements the pipeline described earlier, focusing on the rationale behind key analytical choices and their impact on data quality, statistical reliability, and interpretation of results. By integrating theoretical principles with observations from the outputs, this discussion bridges the analysis steps with the biological and technical context of the study.

\subsection{Parameter Choices and Data Quality}

\textbf{Functional Normalization (\texttt{preprocessFunnorm()})} was selected to correct dye bias, background noise, and partial batch effects, leveraging internal control probes to harmonize data across samples. This choice preserves true biological variability while minimizing technical noise.

\vspace{0.3em}
\noindent\textbf{Detection p-value Cutoff (0.01)} provided a stringent filter to exclude unreliable probes whose signals may not exceed background noise. Although conservative, this threshold ensures that only high-confidence measurements are included, supporting reliable downstream analyses.

\subsection{Quality Control and Normalization}

Negative control and QC plots confirmed consistently low background fluorescence across samples, indicating that no sample exhibited technical anomalies. Density plots comparing raw and normalized data demonstrated that normalization effectively aligned intensity distributions, correcting systematic biases and enabling accurate group comparisons.

\subsection{Methylation Distributions}

Beta-value and M-value density plots displayed the expected bimodal distributions, indicating clear separation between methylated and unmethylated states and confirming the biological relevance of the data.

\subsection{Principal Component Analysis (PCA)}

PCA plots revealed clear separation between control and disease groups, with minimal batch and sex effects, demonstrating that potential confounding variables were adequately controlled.

\subsection{Differential Methylation and Multiple Testing}

The volcano plot highlighted CpGs with substantial methylation differences and significant adjusted p-values, supporting the presence of biologically relevant differential methylation. The p-value distribution plot confirmed a uniform distribution under the null hypothesis, with enrichment at low p-values consistent with true biological signals.

\subsubsection*{P-Value Boxplots (Raw, BH, Bonferroni)}

Boxplots comparing raw, BH-adjusted, and Bonferroni-adjusted p-values illustrated the impact of multiple testing correction: while many CpGs appeared significant using raw p-values, only a subset remained significant after correction, emphasizing the importance of controlling false discovery rates.

\vspace{0.3em}
\noindent\textbf{Note:} In this analysis, a nominal p-value threshold of 0.05 was adopted for the initial t-tests, even though the detection p-value cutoff was set at 0.01 during the quality control phase. This nominal threshold (0.05) is a conventional standard in hypothesis testing that balances sensitivity and specificity prior to applying multiple testing corrections (BH and Bonferroni). The detection p-value cutoff (0.01), on the other hand, is used earlier in the pipeline to filter out unreliable probes and is not directly linked to the statistical threshold applied in the differential testing step.

\subsection{Genome-Wide Overview}

The Manhattan plot visualized genome-wide significance, showing clusters of differentially methylated CpGs at specific chromosomal locations, supporting downstream biological interpretation.

\subsection{Clustering and Heatmaps}

Heatmaps using complete, average, and single linkage methods consistently grouped samples by biological condition (CTRL vs. DIS). The color gradient from green to red effectively represented the continuum from hypomethylation to hypermethylation.

\subsection{Limitations and Potential Improvements}

While the chosen parameters provided a strong analytical foundation, several refinements could be explored. A detection p-value cutoff of 0.01 is conservative and may exclude borderline CpGs with potential biological relevance; testing a slightly higher threshold (e.g., 0.05) could help balance stringency and inclusiveness. Alternative normalization methods, such as quantile normalization or SWAN, might perform differently depending on dataset characteristics. Although batch effects appeared minimal in PCA, additional correction using methods like ComBat could be tested to further reduce potential technical variance. Exploring different probe selections or group assignments might also assess the robustness of the findings under alternative analytical settings.


\section{Function Reference}

\subsection*{Data Import}
\begin{functionbox}
\texttt{read.metharray.exp()} — Imports raw .idat files into an RGChannelSet object.
\end{functionbox}

\subsection*{Annotation}
\begin{functionbox}
\texttt{Illumina450Manifest\_clean} — Cleaned manifest file for probe annotation.
\end{functionbox}

\subsection*{Quality Control}
\begin{functionbox}
\texttt{detectionP()} — Computes detection p-values per probe.\\
\texttt{plotQC()} — Visualizes sample quality metrics.\\
\texttt{controlStripPlot()} — Visualizes negative control probes.
\end{functionbox}

\subsection*{Preprocessing}
\begin{functionbox}
\texttt{preprocessRaw()} — Applies background correction.\\
\texttt{preprocessFunnorm()} — Performs functional normalization.
\end{functionbox}

\subsection*{Beta/M-value Calculation}
\begin{functionbox}
\texttt{getBeta()} — Calculates Beta-values.\\
\texttt{getM()} — Calculates M-values.
\end{functionbox}

\subsection*{Statistical Testing}
\begin{functionbox}
\texttt{t.test()} — Tests for group differences.
\end{functionbox}

\subsection*{Multiple Testing Correction}
\begin{functionbox}
\texttt{p.adjust()} — Adjusts p-values for multiple testing (BH, Bonferroni).
\end{functionbox}

\subsection*{Visualization}
\begin{functionbox}
\texttt{heatmap.2()} — Creates heatmaps with clustering.\\
\texttt{fviz\_eig()} — Plots PCA eigenvalues.\\
\texttt{manhattan()} — Generates Manhattan plots.
\end{functionbox}


\section{Tips and Troubleshooting}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Object Not Found}: Check spelling carefully (case-sensitive) and ensure that the object was created in the current R session. Use \texttt{ls()} to list all objects in the workspace.
    \item \textbf{Package Not Loaded}: Always load packages with \texttt{library()} before using their functions. For Bioconductor packages, remember to install via \texttt{BiocManager::install()} first.
    \item \textbf{Dimension Mismatch}: Check that data frames or matrices used in functions have the same number of rows/columns or match the expected structure. Use \texttt{dim()} and \texttt{head()} to inspect.
    \item \textbf{Warning Messages}: R issues warnings (e.g. \texttt{Warning: NAs introduced by coercion}) that do not stop code but may indicate potential problems. Review warnings carefully with \texttt{warnings()} and adjust code accordingly.
    \item \textbf{Missing Values (NAs)}: Check for missing values with \texttt{is.na()} and decide on appropriate handling (e.g. remove or impute).
    \item \textbf{Normalization Issues}: If downstream analyses seem off (e.g. unexpected sample clustering or dye bias), verify that background correction and normalization have been correctly applied. Re-check steps like \texttt{preprocessRaw()} and \texttt{preprocessFunnorm()}.
    \item \textbf{Dye Bias}: A technical artifact especially in Infinium II probes. Use appropriate normalization methods to correct (e.g. functional normalization).
    \item \textbf{Version Conflicts}: Make sure R and packages are updated to avoid deprecated functions. Check package version with \texttt{packageVersion("packagename")}.
    \item \textbf{Visualization Errors}: If plots look wrong or incomplete, check that the data is correctly structured and complete. Use \texttt{ggplot2} syntax carefully.
    \item \textbf{Unexpected Results}: Always verify that the correct samples and group labels were assigned during preprocessing. Use \texttt{table()} to cross-check factors like group and batch.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.3cm}


\section{Conclusion}
This manual is designed to guide users through the process of DNA methylation data analysis, from data import to visualization and interpretation. Our project demonstrates the critical role of bioinformatics in modern molecular biology and highlights the power of reproducible research. By using robust tools such as R and the minfi package, we can process large datasets efficiently and gain valuable insights into epigenetic regulation and disease mechanisms.

The expected results include the identification of differentially methylated regions (DMRs) that may serve as biomarkers for disease, as well as the discovery of new epigenetic patterns associated with clinical outcomes. This work contributes to the broader goal of personalized medicine, where DNA methylation signatures can inform both diagnostics and therapeutic strategies.

We hope this document empowers other researchers to replicate our pipeline and adapt it to their own datasets, fostering collaboration and accelerating discoveries in the field of epigenetics.

